[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2609092100-a-port-is-not-a-protocol-the-language-lacks"
  :adr/title "A port is not a protocol the language lacks: closed-world dispatch runs, open-world dispatch is refused"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-09"
  :adr/related "adr-2608650000 q9-migration.edn ADR-q9-whole-component-build-migration"
  :adr/body
  "## Decision

`kotoba.card.actuation` and `kotoba.card.ports` are recorded `:blocked` for the
Kotoba migration. The block is asserted by a probe, not written down as a claim:
`kotoba-lang/card` `test/kotoba/card/actuation_kotoba_blocked_probe.cljs`.

## What was measured, in both directions (2026-09-09)

Protocols are NOT missing from the language. A `defprotocol` + `extend-protocol`
whose implementations sit in the same compiled graph COMPILES and RUNS and returns
the right answer -- the probe instantiates the emitted artifact and checks it
returns 103, rather than reading `:ok true`, because a backend that accepts and
answers wrongly is worse than one that refuses (amu#835).

What is refused is the PORT shape: a protocol method called on a value whose record
the module does not statically know.

  :kotoba.error/protocol-dispatch
  'protocol method requires a statically known implemented record; got :i64'

`kotoba-lang/lang/surface-status.edn` names the profile:
`:protocol-and-record-dispatch` is `:bounded-closed-world-static-dispatch`, with
`:dynamic-fallback false` and `:default :sealed-module-record-specialization`.

## Why these two components are exactly that shape

Their implementers live outside the compiled graph ON PURPOSE, and in other
repositories. `io.stripe.issuing.core/StripeIssuing` is a JVM record holding a live
HTTP transport; `cardissuing.http` dispatches on an actuator injected at runtime.
An open world is the whole point of a port. `kotoba.card.ports` is five protocol
declarations and nothing else.

## This is backend state, not a permanent design decision

`:disposition` is `:implemented-partial`, which ADR-2608650000 classifies as
TEMPORARY -- the permanent dispositions are `:intentional-security-constraint` and
`:intentional-semantic-simplification`, and neither is on this profile. So the
components are NOT reshaped to fit the backend.

## The trap this ADR exists to disarm

That surface-status entry carries `:missing []` and `:not-yet-implemented #{}`.
Both are scoped to the closed-world profile. Neither means protocols are complete,
and neither is a statement that open-world dispatch is refused by design. Read as
the latter -- which is the natural reading of `:missing []` -- this block looks
permanent, and a future agent would either stop trying or, worse, redesign a
component around a gap that is scheduled to close.

## Why not migrate the pure half

`approval-issues`, `authorised?`, `idempotency-issues`, `state-mapping-complete?`,
`unrepresentable-states`, `refusal` and `precheck` would each move today. Moving
only those is a decision-only slice, and `q9-migration.edn` v3 sets
`:decision-only-slices-allowed false` with `:migration-unit :whole-component`.

Replacing the ports with declared capability imports is the other way a port can
cross (`:native-functionality-crosses :declared-capability-import`), and it is
rejected here for a reason that is not about the compiler: the library is
provider-agnostic on purpose, so a capability that issued a card would have to name
a provider's wire format, which lives in the provider. It would also delete two
public exports that two other repositories implement -- a versioned API decision,
not a migration step.

## Removal condition, mechanised

The probe exits 0 blocked / 1 unblocked / 2 REFUSED, and was discriminated in three
directions before landing: port shape admitted -> 1; reason literal renamed -> 2
(it refuses to attribute the block to a failure it did not name); control answering
wrongly -> 2. The day open-world dispatch is admitted, it goes red for BOTH
namespaces. `kotoba.card.ports` deliberately has no second probe -- two probes
asserting the same refusal drift apart, and the one nobody runs is the one that
goes stale.

## Note on the control

The first attempt at the control-broken variant changed nothing, and the probe
reported a healthy control, which reads exactly like a successful demonstration.
The mutation is now asserted to have applied before it is trusted. This is the
'壊し方を間違えた赤は成功した実演に見える' failure in its mirror form: a mutation
that misses looks like a passing control."}]
